import OpenProblemsLib

/-
NT-A3.9 (Mathlib). Guy A3, De Koninck's questions: with p(k) the smallest prime whose decimal
digits sum to k, is p(k) ≡ 9 (mod 10) for k > 25, ≡ 99 (mod 100) for k > 38, and
≡ 999 (mod 1000) for k > 59? README result: the second and third parts are false,
p(86) = 6999999989 ≡ 89 (mod 100), ≡ 989 (mod 1000); further failures p(104) = 699999999989,
p(137) = 3999999999999989; the mod-10 part holds for every 25 < k < 180 (k not a multiple of 3).

Digit sum: `ds n = (Nat.digits 10 n).sum` (Mathlib). p(k) is stated as Mathlib
`IsLeast {p | p.Prime ∧ ds p = k}`.

Main theorems:
* `A3_9_main`: IsLeast {p prime, digit sum 86} 6999999989, and it is 89 mod 100, 989 mod 1000;
  `A3_9_false`: so the second and third parts fail (at k = 86).
* `A3_9_values`: p(104) = 699999999989 and p(137) = 3999999999999989, both 89 mod 100 and
  989 mod 1000.
* `A3_9_mod10`: for every k with 25 < k < 180 and 3 ∤ k, p(k) exists and p(k) ≡ 9 (mod 10).
* `A3_9_mult3`: for k > 3 with 3 ∣ k no prime has digit sum k (so p(k) does not exist).

Proof. `table` lists, for each such k, p(k) and a list of factors. Primality: Pratt
certificate. Minimality: `chkF P P k f` is a kernel-evaluated enumeration of all n < P with
digit sum k, digit by digit from the right, pruned by an upper bound on the digit sums below
a bound (`mbF`); `chkF_spec` proves it sound, and `f = comp fs` checks that each such n has a
proper factor in fs. The table data (p(k), factors) is computed outside by
certificate/a39_lean_gen.py and checked here by the kernel.

Outside Lean: nothing for the stated range. The mod-10 question for k ≥ 180 is open (README).
(Generated by certificate/a39_lean_gen.py from certificate/A3_9_mathlib.lean.tmpl.)
-/

